This paper investigates the normalized ground state solutions for the given biharmonic Choquard equation, ( $\left.
\begin{array}{c}
-\Delta ^2u+\beta\Delta u+a(x)u=f(x)\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}
|x|^{-\mu}|u|^p\left|v\right|^q\mathrm{d}x
\right)v, \\
u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4), \|u\|_{L^2}=1
\end{array}
\right.) $, where the function $\beta$ is a real number, and both $f$ and $g$ are functions that satisfy specific conditions within a particular function space, and their exponents $p$ and $q$ meet a particular criterion with given constants. In order to establish the existence of normalized ground state solutions, a novel method is employed to handle the lack of compactness resulting from this critical situation.
